Two changes make the invalid state perceivable to AT: expose `aria-invalid` on the `radiogroup` itself (reflecting `validity`, not just presence of `error`), and give the error text an `id` that's wired up via `aria-describedby` so it's discoverable outside the one-shot `aria-live` announcement.

```js
updated(changedProperties) {
  if (changedProperties.has('error')) {
    // existing behaviour: mirror the error attribute onto each child
    this.items.forEach((el) => {
      if (this.error) el.setAttribute('error', '');
      else el.removeAttribute('error');
    });
  }

  if (changedProperties.has('validity') || changedProperties.has('error')) {
    const invalid = this.validity && this.validity !== 'valid';
    this.items.forEach((el) => {
      el.setAttribute('aria-invalid', invalid ? 'true' : 'false');
    });
  }
}

render() {
  const invalid = this.validity && this.validity !== 'valid';

  return html`
    <fieldset
      role="radiogroup"
      aria-invalid="${invalid ? 'true' : 'false'}"
      aria-describedby="${this.error ? 'group-error' : nothing}"
    >
      <legend><slot name="legend"></slot></legend>
      <slot></slot>
      ${this.error
        ? html`<p id="group-error" role="alert" aria-live="assertive">${this.error}</p>`
        : nothing}
    </fieldset>
  `;
}
```

Why both: `aria-invalid` on the `radiogroup` is what a screen reader announces on focus/entry (e.g. "invalid" in VoiceOver/NVDA), independent of whether the user happened to be listening when the `aria-live` region fired. `aria-describedby` → `group-error` ensures the error text is read out whenever the group is focused/re-visited, not just at the moment it first appears. The `aria-live="assertive"` region is still useful for the initial interruption when validation first fails.
